INT. NIGHT LAUNDROMAT - 2 A.M.

Buzzing tubes. Twelve machines, all but two idle. A vending machine glows. Rain at the glass. Lint in the corners like snow.

IMOGEN LAU, 30s, scrubs under a trench coat, sits on a folding table. Boots swinging. A paperback open and unread. Her dryer turns a single blue sock.

SULEIMAN BAH, 30s, tall, soft cardigan, loads a washer. Careful with each shirt. He folds one before he puts it in.

Silence.

Her eyes flick to him. His hands pause on a collar. Resume.

IMOGEN
You fold before you wash.

SULEIMAN
Habit.

IMOGEN
Strange habit.

SULEIMAN
Strange hours.

She turns a page she hasn't read. He shuts the washer. Coins. Click. Water starts.

IMOGEN
You come here often?

SULEIMAN
Tuesdays.

IMOGEN
It's Thursday.

He looks at the dryer. At the single blue sock.

SULEIMAN
Missed a Tuesday.

She nods. Watches the sock tumble. He leans against the washer. Not too close. Not far.

IMOGEN
Night shift?

SULEIMAN
Nurse. You?

IMOGEN
Same.

SULEIMAN
Which floor?

IMOGEN
Fourth.

SULEIMAN
Third.

A beat. The washer sloshes. The vending machine hums. She taps her book.

IMOGEN
We've never met.

SULEIMAN
We passed on the stairs. Once.

IMOGEN
Did we?

SULEIMAN
You had a croissant.

She almost smiles. Hides it with the book. He picks a coin from his palm and rolls it across his knuckles.

IMOGEN
That was a bad day.

SULEIMAN
It was a good croissant.

The dryer buzzes. She hops down. Opens it. Pulls out the sock. Looks at it. At him.

IMOGEN
Don't suppose you've lost one.

He reaches into his cardigan pocket. Pulls out a blue sock. The match.

They hold the two socks side by side. Neither says anything.

IMOGEN (CONT'D)
Tuesday?

SULEIMAN
Tuesday.

She sets her sock in his palm. Their fingers do not touch. Almost. She picks up her book and walks out into the rain without her coat.
